issue(id="0006" status="closed")
  title Add distinct handling for Pug comments and filter blocks
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of the puggers formatter
    i-want comments and filters modeled explicitly
    so-that the formatter can preserve intent and eventually apply syntax-aware rules to those constructs
  premise.
    The formatter currently flattens comments into a simple `//` form and uses a
    generic dotted-block rule for many block-like constructs. That loses the
    distinction between buffered versus unbuffered Pug comments and leaves
    filter blocks like `:markdown` or `:javascript` without a real syntax model.
    These are common enough in the language that they deserve explicit
    representation before the formatter grows more aggressive policies.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Represent `//` and `//-` distinctly in the AST and formatter behavior.
    criterion Introduce explicit handling for filter blocks such as `:markdown`, `:css`, and `:javascript`.
    criterion Preserve filter payloads losslessly even if embedded-language formatting is out of scope initially.
    criterion Add tests covering comments at different nesting levels and representative filter blocks from real-world Pug usage.
  review-items
    item(status="covered") Filters remain opaque payloads by default; any future host-formatter delegation should come later as an explicit opt-in capability rather than baseline behavior.
    item(status="covered") Comment heads now normalize to a readable `// ` or `//- ` form while still preserving the buffered versus unbuffered distinction; stricter authored-spacing preservation is intentionally deferred unless a later task finds user-visible breakage.
    item(status="covered") Filters now use explicit parser and formatter handling instead of relying on the generic dotted-block heuristic, with dedicated outcome-first coverage for nested and blank-line-sensitive payloads.
